  "slug": "genome-aware-variant-aaa5",
  "summary": "Body models, brain atlases and surface-deformation networks now yield quantitative readouts of human structure and motion.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantitative, model-based mapping of human anatomy and movement. The preprint reads clinical joint angles directly from per-segment rotation matrices of parametric body models (GEM-X, SAM 3D Body) on single-smartphone video, bypassing inverse kinematics. The PLoS Computational Biology study applies community detection to body surface deformation to identify synchronous trunk deformation regions during gait and reports trunk modularity. Siibra, published in Nature Methods, is a software suite that links cloud brain data to reference atlases through a web viewer, Python library and HTTP API to realize a multilevel human brain atlas. The items differ in scale and modality, spanning whole-body surface, gait mechanics and brain atlases, so the shared element is structured, quantitative reference representation rather than a common method. The new element in the preprint is deriving clinical angles from model rotation matrices without an intermediate kinematic fitting step.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-26"
}
